        { id: "s13", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  

  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage — Why This Separate Provision Is Legally Necessary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout suc">CLOSING BOOK 3, SECTION 2 ENTIRELY: §335b is the final provision of the entire criminal/administrative/coercive-fine Subsection this poster series has documented across §§331–335a — and, with it, the final provision of Book 3, Section 2 (Supplementary Provisions for Corporations) as a whole. A short, purely extending provision — but a legally NECESSARY one, for reasons rooted in constitutional criminal-law principle.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">A closing of a genuine gap, required by criminal-law certainty principles</div>
      <div class="principle">Recall §264a from much earlier in this series: certain OHG/KG partnerships lacking any personally-liable natural person (the classic GmbH &amp; Co. KG structure) must follow the SAME corporation-level accounting rules as a genuine Kapitalgesellschaft. But German constitutional criminal-law principle (nulla poena sine lege certa — no punishment without a sufficiently definite law) requires that criminal and administrative sanctions have their OWN explicit, sufficiently specific statutory basis — §264a's own extension of ACCOUNTING obligations does NOT automatically carry criminal or administrative CONSEQUENCES along with it. §335b is precisely this necessary, separate extension.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §335B MAPS TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Bestimmtheitsgrundsatz (the principle of legal certainty)</strong> — a genuinely foundational German constitutional-law concept requiring criminal/administrative liability to rest on its OWN clear statutory foundation</span>
        <span><strong>Substance over form, closing the loop</strong> — the SAME anti-avoidance logic that originally justified §264a itself (denying the escape-hatch of formal partnership status where no genuine personal liability exists) is completed here for the enforcement dimension too</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §335b does, at a glance</div>
      <div class="principle">§335b extends the ENTIRE criminal-provisions cluster (§§331–333a), the administrative fine provision (§334), and the coercive fine provision (§335) — plus, by cross-reference, the appeal mechanism of §335a — to OHG and KG partnerships within the meaning of §264a(1), with specific adaptations to who exactly the proceedings are directed against.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — §264a(1) Partnerships Specifically</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The exact same population established many posters ago</div>
      <div class="principle">§335b applies to the SAME OHG and KG partnerships §264a(1) already established — those where NO natural person bears unlimited personal liability, typically because the sole general partner (Komplementär) is itself a limited-liability entity, most commonly a GmbH. Recall the worked example from that earlier poster: KG Beta, with a GmbH as its sole general partner, was caught by §264a where KG Alpha, with a natural-person general partner bearing genuine personal liability, was not — the SAME distinction governs §335b's own scope.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Core Extension — Every Provision From §331 Through §335</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A complete, comprehensive extension — nothing in this cluster is left out</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>§§331–333a</strong> — the entire criminal-provisions cluster: misrepresentation, false assurance (Bilanzeid), auditor accuracy/confidentiality breaches, and the audit committee bribery/persistence offence</span>
        <span><strong>§334</strong> — the negligence-level administrative fine provision, including its own massive cross-reference list of substantive violations</span>
        <span><strong>§335</strong> — the disclosure-enforcement Ordnungsgeldverfahren, with its own escalating, repeating structure and size-sensitive late-compliance reductions</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">This is a genuinely COMPREHENSIVE extension — every single provision this poster series has documented across the entire Straf-/Bußgeld-/Ordnungsgeld Subsection applies with EQUAL force to a qualifying partnership as it would to an ordinary Kapitalgesellschaft.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Worked Example — a GmbH &amp; Co. KG's General Partner Facing §331 Exposure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">The same deliberate falsification, the same criminal consequence, in a partnership wrapper</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Deliberate misrepresentation at a GmbH &amp; Co. KG</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A GmbH &amp; Co. KG — caught by §264a(1) since its sole general partner is itself a GmbH with no natural person bearing personal liability anywhere in the structure — has its accounts deliberately falsified by the general-partner GmbH's own management, to flatter results ahead of a financing round, exactly paralleling the Example 1 scenario from the very first §331 poster in this series.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the entity being, in legal FORM, a partnership rather than a Kapitalgesellschaft, §335b extends §331's own criminal exposure — imprisonment up to three years or a fine — to this scenario in FULL, exactly as it would apply to an ordinary corporation. The partnership structure provides NO shelter from the same criminal accountability this series has documented throughout.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The Adapted Täterkreis — Personally Liable Partners, or Their Representative Bodies</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A sensible adaptation for an entity that has no board of its own</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§335b sentence 2 specifically adapts §335's own Ordnungsgeldverfahren mechanism to be directed against the PERSONALLY LIABLE PARTNERS (persönlich haftende Gesellschafter) of the qualifying OHG/KG, OR against the MEMBERS of the REPRESENTATIVE BODIES of those personally liable partners — recognising that a partnership itself has no management or supervisory board in the same sense a corporation does, so the proceeding must instead reach whoever ACTUALLY exercises equivalent authority: typically, the management board of the GmbH serving as the general partner.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Applying the Adapted Täterkreis</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Reaching through the general partner to the individuals who actually manage it</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Identifying who a §335 proceeding actually targets</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A GmbH &amp; Co. KG fails to disclose its Jahresabschluss on time. The KG's sole general partner is "Komplementär-GmbH," whose own management board consists of two individuals.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The §335 Ordnungsgeldverfahren, as extended by §335b, is directed against EITHER the Komplementär-GmbH itself (as the personally liable partner) OR directly against its TWO management board members individually — reaching THROUGH the corporate general-partner structure to the actual individuals exercising management authority, rather than being stymied by the KG's own lack of a traditional board.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>The Dual-Target Structure, Continued</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The same dual-track approach established for ordinary corporations</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§335b sentence 3 confirms the SAME dual-target structure already established for ordinary Kapitalgesellschaften under §335(1): the proceeding MAY ALSO be directed against the OHG or KG itself directly, not only against the personally liable partners or their representative bodies — giving the Bundesamt genuine flexibility in how it pursues enforcement.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§335a's Extension — the Appeal Mechanism Too</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A qualifying partnership enjoys the exact same procedural protections</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§335b sentence 4 confirms that §335a — the two-tier appeal structure this series just documented in full, with its two-week Beschwerde deadline, suspensive effect, LG Bonn's exclusive jurisdiction, and narrow, permission-gated Rechtsbeschwerde — applies CORRESPONDINGLY here too. A qualifying partnership faces neither MORE nor LESS enforcement exposure, and enjoys the exact SAME procedural protections, as an ordinary Kapitalgesellschaft would.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Confirming a specific partnership genuinely qualifies under §264a(1) before proceeding with any §335b-extended enforcement action</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Correctly identifying the appropriate individual targets within a multi-tier general-partner structure, particularly where the general partner is itself part of a larger corporate group</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Entity-type-specific enforcement extension sits entirely outside any accounting standard</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Precisely as with every other provision in this criminal/administrative cluster, IFRS has no equivalent — the specific question of WHICH entity types a jurisdiction's own enforcement apparatus reaches is a matter of purely domestic company and criminal law. §335b completes this poster series' documentation of just how comprehensively German law closes potential gaps in its own accountability architecture — leaving no realistic legal-form loophole through which a genuinely corporation-like enterprise could escape the same accountability an actual corporation faces.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §335b event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Any §331–335 violation, at a §264a partnership</span><span><span class="badge bo">Jahresabschluss / disclosure filing</span></span><span>Identical consequences to an ordinary Kapitalgesellschaft</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §335b connects across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§264a(1)</span><span>Defines the exact population §335b extends this entire cluster to</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§§331–333a, §334, §335, §335a</span><span>The complete cluster this provision extends in full</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§335c</span><span>The NEXT and final provision of this entire Book 3, Section 2 — notifications to the auditor oversight body</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout suc">With §335b, this poster series has now documented the ENTIRE Straf-, Bußgeld- und Ordnungsgeldvorschriften Subsection — the last major substantive cluster of Book 3, Section 2. One final, narrow provision (§335c) remains before this Section closes entirely.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Nulla poena sine lege certa</div><div class="glossary-def">"No punishment without a sufficiently definite law" — the German constitutional principle requiring §335b's separate, explicit extension.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Persönlich haftender Gesellschafter</div><div class="glossary-def">Personally liable partner — the adapted Täterkreis target for a qualifying partnership's own §335 proceeding.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Komplementär-GmbH</div><div class="glossary-def">A GmbH serving as the sole general partner of a KG, the classic structure triggering §264a(1) and hence §335b.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
